{"name":"Blue Grouse","hex":"#9abcdc","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue grouse","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dusky grouse, a bird of species (Dendragapus obscurus), once thought to inhabit both the Rocky Mountains and the Pacific coastal ranges of western North America, originally thought to be the same species as the now-segregate species Dendragapus fuliginosus (sooty grouse), of the Pacific Coast ranges."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_grouse"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dendragapus","blocks":[{"text":"The genus Dendragapus contains two closely related species of grouse that have often been treated as a single variable taxon. The two species are the dusky grouse and the sooty grouse. In addition, the spruce grouse and Siberian grouse have been considered part of this genus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Dendragapus_obscurus_USNPS.jpg/330px-Dendragapus_obscurus_USNPS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dendragapus"},{"title":"Grouse","blocks":[{"text":"Grouse are a group of birds from the order Galliformes, in the family Phasianidae. Grouse are presently assigned to the tribe Tetraonini, a classification supported by mitochondrial DNA sequence studies, and applied by the AviList, International Ornithological Congress, the American Ornithologists' Union, and others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Metsis_ehk_m%C3%B5tus_%28Tetrao_urogallus%29%2C_Euroopa_suurim_kanaline_2012.jpg/330px-Metsis_ehk_m%C3%B5tus_%28Tetrao_urogallus%29%2C_Euroopa_suurim_kanaline_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grouse"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5523.JPG/500px-Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5523.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5523.JPG/500px-Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5523.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Females of both species (sooty grouse pictured) are mottled brown with dark brown and white marks on the underparts.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADendragapus_fuliginosus_5523.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5076.JPG/500px-Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5076.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5076.JPG/1280px-Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5076.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5076.JPG/500px-Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5076.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In breeding plumage, this sooty grouse male is typical of the species. It is dark grey with a yellow wattle over the eye. The tail is long and black with a square pale gray tip.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADendragapus_fuliginosus_5076.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/084_-_DUSKY_GROUSE_%288-23-12%29_uncompahgre_nat_for%2C_gunnison_co%2C_co_%284%29_%288719896661%29.jpg/500px-084_-_DUSKY_GROUSE_%288-23-12%29_uncompahgre_nat_for%2C_gunnison_co%2C_co_%284%29_%288719896661%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/084_-_DUSKY_GROUSE_%288-23-12%29_uncompahgre_nat_for%2C_gunnison_co%2C_co_%284%29_%288719896661%29.jpg/1280px-084_-_DUSKY_GROUSE_%288-23-12%29_uncompahgre_nat_for%2C_gunnison_co%2C_co_%284%29_%288719896661%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/084_-_DUSKY_GROUSE_%288-23-12%29_uncompahgre_nat_for%2C_gunnison_co%2C_co_%284%29_%288719896661%29.jpg/500px-084_-_DUSKY_GROUSE_%288-23-12%29_uncompahgre_nat_for%2C_gunnison_co%2C_co_%284%29_%288719896661%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A084_-_DUSKY_GROUSE_(8-23-12)_uncompahgre_nat_for%2C_gunnison_co%2C_co_(4)_(8719896661).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5058.JPG/500px-Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5058.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5058.JPG/1280px-Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5058.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5058.JPG/500px-Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5058.JPG?utm_source=en.w